FILE: app/src/mode-limits.ts
```ts
import type { GeospatialCamera, GeospatialLimits } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function useModeLimits(camera: GeospatialCamera, limits: GeospatialLimits): void {
    camera.limits = limits;
    // Force the camera to re-clamp its current pose against the new limits
    // immediately (note 37: a center assignment, even self-assignment, triggers
    // the limits change to apply).
    camera.center = camera.center;
}
```

I wrote `src/mode-limits.ts` with a two-line function: it assigns the mode's `GeospatialLimits` object to `camera.limits` (so `camera.limits` reports the correct values and all subsequent radius/yaw writes are clamped by the camera's built-in mechanism), then performs a self-assignment of `camera.center` to force the camera to re-clamp its current pose against the new limits immediately rather than waiting for the next value-changing write. I was not sure of anything — the notes (particularly 37, 54, and 59) make the behavior unambiguous.
